  "title": "Bean Scope",
  "category": "Spring Framework",
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "what-is-a-bean-scope-in-spring",
      "heading": "What is a Bean Scope in Spring?",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Definition: In Spring, a bean scope defines the lifecycle and visibility of a bean within the context of the application. It determines how many instances of a bean are created and how they are shared or accessed.",
            "In simple terms: Bean scopes determine how and when new bean objects are created by the Spring IoC Container."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 56,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "id": "lifecycle-vs-scope",
          "heading": "Lifecycle vs Scope",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Lifecycle: Refers to the stages a bean goes through (creation → initialization → use → destruction). 👉 Note: Detailed lifecycle stages are out of scope for this course.",
                "Scope: Refers to how long a bean lives and who can access it during its lifecycle."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 44,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "design-patterns-behind-bean-scopes",
      "heading": "Design Patterns Behind Bean Scopes",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [],
      "wordCount": 0,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "id": "1-singleton-pattern",
          "heading": "1. Singleton Pattern",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Definition: Only one instance of the class is created and used throughout the application.",
                "Use Cases: Logging, configuration, caching, database connections.",
                "Spring Mapping: Implemented using singleton scope.",
                "Characteristics: "
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 28,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "2-prototype-pattern",
          "heading": "2. Prototype Pattern",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Definition: A new instance of the class is created for every request.",
                "Use Cases: UI components, dynamic forms, custom user sessions.",
                "Spring Mapping: Implemented using prototype scope.",
                "Characteristics: "
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 28,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "available-bean-scopes-in-spring",
      "heading": "Available Bean Scopes in Spring",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Scope",
            "Description"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "singleton",
              "Single instance shared across entire Spring container (Default)."
            ],
            [
              "prototype",
              "New instance for every request."
            ],
            [
              "request",
              "One instance per HTTP request (Web Applications only)."
            ],
            [
              "session",
              "One instance per HTTP session (Web Applications only)."
            ],
            [
              "application",
              "One instance per ServletContext."
            ],
            [
              "websocket",
              "One instance per WebSocket connection."
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "📌 Note:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Singleton and Prototype are applicable in any Spring application (standalone or web).",
            "Others are limited to Spring Web Context."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 67,
      "subsections": []
    },
